5. End-to-end verification: server + client write/read at arbitrary offsets, verify tree layout on disk; regression of normal export

Now let's write the code.

### treefiles.c

```c
/*
 * Tree file support for nbd-server.
 *
 * A treefiles export stores device data in a tree of small block
 * files: each file holds TREEPAGESIZE bytes of data, each directory
 * holds at most TREEDIRSIZE entries, and the nesting depth is chosen
 * so the tree can hold the full export size. This allows exporting
 * a large virtual block device on filesystems that do not handle
 * large files well (fuse/ftpfs, davfs, other network filesystems).
 *
 * The layout is documented in nbd-server(5), under "treefiles".
 */

#include "lfs.h"

#include <errno.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <unistd.h>

#include "treefiles.h"

/* Maximum number of subdirectory levels. Each level multiplies
 * the capacity of the tree by TREEDIRSIZE, so six levels can
 * address TREEDIRSIZE^7 blocks, which is far more than can fit
 * in a device whose size fits in off_t. */
#define TREE_MAXLEVELS 6

/*
 * Compute the path of the block file that holds the data at offset
 * pos of a device of size bytes, below the tree rooted at name.
 *
 * name is an in/out buffer of lenmax bytes: it must contain the tree's
 * root directory on entry, and will contain the full path of the block
 * file on return. *ppos is set to the offset within the block file.
 */
void construct_path(char *name, int lenmax, off_t size, off_t pos, off_t *ppos) {
	off_t blocks = (size + TREEPAGESIZE - 1) / TREEPAGESIZE;
	off_t block = pos / TREEPAGESIZE;
	off_t capacity = TREEDIRSIZE;
	off_t divisor;
	int levels = 0;
	int len = strlen(name);
	int i;

	if (ppos) {
		*ppos = pos % TREEPAGESIZE;
	}

	while (capacity < blocks && levels < TREE_MAXLEVELS) {
		capacity *= TREEDIRSIZE;
		levels++;
	}

	/* Decompose the block number into levels+1 digits in base
	 * TREEDIRSIZE, most significant first. The first levels
	 * digits select a subdirectory at each level, the last one
	 * selects the block file within its directory. */
	divisor = capacity / TREEDIRSIZE;
	for (i = 0; i < levels; i++) {
		len += snprintf(name + len, lenmax - len, "/TREE%04d",
				(int)((block / divisor) % TREEDIRSIZE));
		divisor /= TREEDIRSIZE;
	}
	snprintf(name + len, lenmax - len, "/FILE%04d",
		 (int)(block % TREEDIRSIZE));
}
```

Hmm, wait, subtle point about the divisor computation: capacity = TREEDIRSIZE^(levels+1). divisor starts at TREEDIRSIZE^levels = capacity/TREEDIRSIZE. At each directory level i, digit = (block / TREEDIRSIZE^(levels-i)) % TREEDIRSIZE. Let's verify: levels=2, capacity=TREEDIRSIZE^3, divisor=TREEDIRSIZE^2. i=0: digit = (block / TREEDIRSIZE^2) % TREEDIRSIZE ✓; divisor /= TREEDIRSIZE → TREEDIRSIZE^1. i=1: digit = (block / TREEDIRSIZE^1) % TREEDIRSIZE ✓. File index = block % TREEDIRSIZE ✓.

snprintf returns the number of characters that *would have been* written; if truncation occurs, len could exceed lenmax, and lenmax - len becomes negative → does snprintf handle negative size? size_t cast → huge value. Dangerous. snprintf(name+len, lenmax-len,...) — if len >= lenmax, size underflows. Guard: after each step `if (len >= lenmax) return;`... but the void function still has to produce something. The buffer is sized by the caller; on truncation the path becomes garbage and open will fail → I/O error. Acceptable, but avoid UB. Let's clamp:

```c
static int append_component(char *name, int lenmax, int len, const char *fmt, int digit) {
	int rv;
	if (len >= lenmax)
		return len;
	rv = snprintf(name + len, lenmax - len, fmt, digit);
	if (rv > 0)
		len += rv;
	return len;
}
```

Still truncates, but no UB. Good enough; the caller uses a sufficiently large buffer (compute exact upper bound).

mkdir_path:

```c
/*
 * Create all directories in the given path, which is interpreted as
 * the path of a file: all components up to but not including the last
 * one are created as directories if they do not already exist.
 */
void mkdir_path(char *path) {
	char *sep;

	sep = strchr(path + 1, '/');
	while (sep) {
		*sep = '\0';
		mkdir(path, 0700);
		*sep = '/';
		sep = strchr(sep + 1, '/');
	}
}
```

Ignore mkdir errors: EEXIST is fine; other errors (EACCES, ENOENT for a missing ancestor? no — we create ancestors in order) will surface later at open(). Actually, if mkdir fails with something other than EEXIST (e.g., ENOSPC), open will fail with ENOENT/EACCES — the error surfaces via the return value of open_treefile. errno will be from open, not from mkdir... acceptable; though we could preserve errno. Keep it simple. Hmm, one point: `mkdir(path, 0700)` — what mode does the project use for files? open with 0600. Directories are 0700. OK.

Also skip the leading '/' — strchr(path+1, '/') handles absolute paths; for relative paths like "foo/bar", the first sep is the first '/' — mkdir("foo") ✓.

open_treefile:

```c
/*
 * Open the block file holding data at offset pos of a device of size
 * bytes in the tree rooted at name.
 *
 * If mode includes O_CREAT, missing directories and the block file
 * itself are created; otherwise, -1 is returned with errno set to
 * ENOENT if the block file does not exist yet. mutex serialises tree
 * modifications so that concurrent threads do not race to create the
 * same directories and files.
 *
 * Returns a file descriptor opened with mode, or -1 on error.
 */
int open_treefile(char *name, mode_t mode, off_t size, off_t pos, pthread_mutex_t *mutex) {
	/* enough for TREE_MAXLEVELS "TREEXXXX" components plus a
	 * "FILEXXXX" component, each prefixed with '/' and NUL-terminated */
	char path[strlen(name) + (TREE_MAXLEVELS + 1) * 10 + 1];
	int fd = -1;

	pthread_mutex_lock(mutex);
	strcpy(path, name);
	construct_path(path, sizeof(path), size, pos, NULL);
	if (mode & O_CREAT) {
		mkdir_path(path);
	}
	fd = open(path, mode, 0600);
	pthread_mutex_unlock(mutex);

	return fd;
}
```

Wait — VLA via strlen: fine in C99/gnu (the project uses gnu C). `mode & O_CREAT` — mode is mode_t; O_CREAT is an open flag. The caller passes open flags. OK.

Hmm, should we hold the mutex over the whole open when not creating? Not required but harmless. Actually — locking on every open including reads serializes read I/O through a single mutex, reducing parallelism; however each operation is just path computation + open, so it's tiny. Fine. Hmm, but actually, should we only lock when creating? If two threads call open_treefile on the same path with O_CREAT simultaneously: mkdir EEXIST is handled, open O_CREAT is atomic. So the lock isn't even strictly needed... but the signature includes a mutex (upstream design), so let's use it. To keep read parallelism, lock only when O_CREAT. Hmm, but then the mutex parameter becomes nearly decorative... whatever: lock only on creation, document it.

Wait, there is actually one real race worth protecting: none. mkdir/open handle EEXIST. The lock is for upstream API compatibility. Lock around mkdir+open when creating.

delete_treefile:

```c
/*
 * Delete the block file holding data at offset pos of a device of
 * size bytes in the tree rooted at name, if it exists.
 */
void delete_treefile(char *name, off_t size, off_t pos) {
	char path[strlen(name) + (TREE_MAXLEVELS + 1) * 10 + 1];

	strcpy(path, name);
	construct_path(path, sizeof(path), size, pos, NULL);
	unlink(path);
}
```

Ignore ENOENT (already absent = already "unused"). void return per the header.

Next — pthread mutex: treefiles.h includes pthread.h ✓.

Note about `mode & O_CREAT`: O_CREAT on Linux is 0100 (octal). mode_t is fine.

