The helper (static, before exptrim):

```c
/**
 * Handle TRIM for treefiles exports. Blocks that are completely
 * contained in the trim range have their block file removed, while
 * for blocks that are partially contained, only the trimmed bytes
 * are discarded.
 **/
static int exptrim_treefiles(struct nbd_request* req, CLIENT* client) {
	off_t from = req->from;
	off_t end = from + (off_t)req->len;
	off_t blk;

	if(req->len == 0)
		return 0;
	for(blk = from / TREEPAGESIZE; blk * TREEPAGESIZE < end; blk++) {
		off_t blkstart = blk * TREEPAGESIZE;

		if(from <= blkstart && blkstart + TREEPAGESIZE <= end) {
			/* Whole block trimmed: remove the file */
			delete_treefile(client->exportname,
					(off_t)client->exportsize, blkstart);
		} else {
			/* Partial block: only discard the trimmed part */
			off_t ppos = from > blkstart ? from - blkstart : 0;
			off_t pend = end < blkstart + TREEPAGESIZE ?
				end : blkstart + TREEPAGESIZE;
			int fhandle = open_treefile(client->exportname,
					O_RDWR, (off_t)client->exportsize,
					blkstart, &client->tree_lock);

			if(fhandle < 0) {
				if(errno == ENOENT)
					continue;
				return -1;
			}
			punch_hole(fhandle, ppos, pend - ppos);
			close(fhandle);
		}
	}
	DEBUG("Performed TRIM request from %llu to %llu", (unsigned long long) req->from, (unsigned long long) req->len);
	return 0;
}
```

Hold on — there's a subtle point in the loop bounds: `blk * TREEPAGESIZE < end` — blk is off_t. Since end ≤ exportsize ≤ OFFT_MAX, there's no problem with overflow.

Also, a note about `req->from + req->len`: req->from and req->len are uint64_t. Casting to off_t is fine since bad_range guarantees ≤ exportsize ≤ OFFT_MAX.

Hmm, also `client->exportname` is char* ✓. `delete_treefile(client->exportname, ...)` — signature is `void delete_treefile(char *name, off_t size, off_t pos)` ✓.

Now, placement of the DEBUG message: the existing exptrim prints DEBUG at the end on the normal path. It's fine for the treefiles path to do its own debug print.

